\title{\rg: Verifiable Guardrails for Natural-Language Explanations\\ from Formal Anomaly Detectors in Safety-Critical Systems}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Ravan Bilalov}
\IEEEauthorblockA{M.Sc. Applied Data Science and Analytics\\
SRH University Hamburg, Germany\\
in collaboration with the Faculty of Information Technology,\\
Brno University of Technology (NES@FIT), Czech Republic\\
\texttt{ravanbilalov03@gmail.com}}}

\maketitle

% ============================================================
\begin{abstract}
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used to turn machine-readable outputs from formal anomaly detectors into natural-language explanations. Although such explanations can be supported by strong guarantees about the underlying anomaly detectors, these guarantees do not extend to the explanations themselves. However, these explanations can be acted upon directly by humans and can be critical for making important decisions in safety-critical settings.

To mitigate these challenges, we propose the novel problem of verifiable explanation faithfulness for safety-critical settings. We introduce a framework for this problem that derives a set of bounds on the claims that an explanation is allowed to make based on a given anomaly detection result, and checks explanations at inference time to see if they stay within these bounds. Our solution, \rg, uses a fail-closed deterministic verifier that can flag deviations from these bounds with explanations of what went wrong. These violations are grouped into five violation classes (V1--V5) that can be mapped to varying levels of severity.

We evaluate \rg{} on a smart-grid network traffic dataset from Brno University of Technology conforming to the IEC-104 protocol, and produce 200 ground-truth annotations with blind human annotation. Our evaluation considers 1000 explanations: 500 produced by three local and two cloud-based LLMs, and 500 synthetic regression cases. Our results show that violation types are statistically associated with both model and event type, revealing model-specific failure profiles obscured by aggregate benchmarking.

Detection is more precise for hallucination violations, with precision of 0.90 and recall of 0.09 for V1 violations, and more sensitive for underspecification, with precision of 0.48 and recall of 0.92 for V4 violations. While some limitations, such as overly broad rules that cause overtriggering, lead to a lower-than-desired agreement with human annotation (Cohen's $\kappa$: 0.16) and position \rg{} as a pre-filtering tool for use within a human-in-the-loop pipeline, our solution is a step towards making LLM-generated explanation faithfulness verifiable with guarantees in critical settings.

\rg{} outputs are also mappable to Article~13 (transparency) and Article~17 (quality management) of the EU AI Act. \rg's code, dataset, and prompts are available for reuse under an open-source license.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
runtime verification, faithfulness, large language models, formal methods, industrial control systems, IEC-104, EU AI Act, explainability
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Consider a detector, for example, based on finite-state automata, that observes messages on a private network used to operate an electrical substation from a control centre. Such a detector provides mathematical guarantees that if it raises an alarm then there is a violation of the tele-control protocol, possibly indicating an attack. However, the verdict of such a detector can be hard to understand. An explanation provided by a large language model (LLM) can make such a verdict easier to understand by rewriting it in terms that are more easily comprehensible for the operator, for example, if she is under time pressure. However, recent studies indicate that LLMs hallucinate: they provide fluent but inaccurate responses that are not supported by the input~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness}. Thus, while the verdict of the original detector is bounded, the final explanation shown to the user is not. Such hallucinations are undesirable since the operator may ignore the explanation and hence a real attack. Alternatively, she may devote precious time to investigate a false alarm. Thus, in this example, the ability of an LLM to provide trustworthy explanations is not just a nice-to-have feature but a safety requirement.

While LLM hallucinations have received much attention, current methods for evaluating LLM output are not fit for such scenarios. Such methods~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness,Min2023FActScore,Honovich2022True} are offline and typically focus on computing an aggregated consistency measure for a collection of inputs and outputs. In contrast, consider that LLM-generated text is consumed online, that is, one explanation at a time as it becomes available. Moreover, in safety-critical scenarios it is essential to have guarantees about the quality of a particular explanation before it is shown to the user. Such guarantees can be obtained by performing runtime verification of LLM outputs, enabling unreliable texts to be hidden, flagged, or regenerated.

This paper presents our approach called \rg{} that uses runtime verification of LLM-generated explanations. \rg{} uses a given explanation's context, for example, the verdict of the aforementioned protocol violation detector, as a machine-readable specification that bounds what should be present in the explanation. \rg{} checks whether a given explanation violates its specification and provides actionable insights about why. Unlike previous work that checks whether LLM-generated texts are faithful to their contexts and returns a single aggregated faithfulness score, \rg{} reports whether texts are faithful and returns fine-grained justifications by identifying which of five violation types applies.

\noindent\textbf{Contributions.}
First, to our knowledge, \rg{} is the first RV framework that checks LLM-generated explanations against a formally verified detector bound: the verifier is deterministic, auditable, and fail-closed. The framework is presented in \cref{sec:framework}.

Second, we propose a reasoning-violation taxonomy of five classes---V1 (fabricated), V2 (contradicted), V3 (over-generalised), V4 (under-specified), and V5 (incoherent)---each with a formal definition, a detection rule, and a severity mapping.

Third, we present a comprehensive empirical study (\cref{sec:results}) on 1,000 explanations built on IEC-104 data---500 produced by five LLMs and 500 synthetic regression cases---analysed against 200 blind human annotations to understand failure patterns across events and models. Our analysis honestly reports the capabilities and the boundaries of our approach, shedding light on the operational limits of such rule-based verification.

Finally, we offer practical guidance for the use of \rg{} for LLM-generated explanations and map it to Articles~13 and 17 of the EU AI Act~\cite{EUAIAct2024}. A fully reproducible open-source artefact is available~\cite{ReasonGuardArtifact2026}.

% ============================================================
\section{Background and Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Formal methods and automaton-based ICS detection}
Model checking verifies whether a finite-state model of a system satisfies a temporal specification~\cite{ClarkeModelChecking2001}. Applied to ICS, the specification describes legitimate behaviour and a violation constitutes a proof of anomaly with respect to the model, not a heuristic score.

Hol\'{i}k et al.~\cite{HolikMatousekRysavyVojnar2023} learn deterministic probabilistic automata from legitimate IEC-104 and MMS traffic and incorporate message semantics to reduce false positives; their detector emits a structured verdict (anomaly type, affected devices, timestamps). \rg{} consumes such a verdict as a specification that explanations must not violate.

Unlike a program, an LLM cannot be bounded and modelled tractably, since its behaviour varies with the prompt and is opaque, so proving a property for all executions is impossible; RV circumvents this by checking a single execution.

\subsection{Faithfulness evaluation}
Faithfulness research distinguishes factuality (with respect to the world) from faithfulness (with respect to a source)~\cite{Maynez2020Faithfulness}.

\textsc{FActScore}~\cite{Min2023FActScore} decomposes generated text into atomic facts and checks each against a knowledge base; \textsc{TRUE}~\cite{Honovich2022True} unifies eleven benchmarks and finds natural-language-inference and question-generation metrics complementary.

\rg{} shares the atomic-claim idea but differs in three ways: it is deterministic and auditable (desirable for certification), it returns an actionable class rather than a scalar, and it checks faithfulness to a formal-methods verdict rather than to a document---an area of limited prior work.

\subsection{Runtime verification}
RV asks whether a single execution satisfies a property while the system runs, using monitors synthesised from formal specifications~\cite{LeuckerSchallhart2009RuntimeVerification}.

\rg{} fits this picture: the execution is the generated explanation, the specification is the formal bound, and the monitor is the verifier.

Faithfulness is a safety property---an explanation may make unboundedly many true assertions, but a single false one refutes it---so a finite input suffices to detect a violation. We contribute a fail-closed bound that rejects any assertion unless it is explicitly permitted, preserving the monitor's independence from the generator.

\subsection{The EU AI Act}
Regulation (EU) 2024/1689~\cite{EUAIAct2024} classifies AI safety components in the operation of critical infrastructure (electricity, gas, water) as high-risk (Annex III).

Article~13 requires sufficient transparency for recipients to interpret outputs; Article~17 requires a documented quality-management system covering testing, record-keeping, monitoring, and corrective action.

\rg{} is not itself a control component, but by monitoring the faithfulness of operator-facing explanations it can produce evidence toward both duties (\cref{sec:discussion}).

