\subsection{Reasoning as assertions}
We operationalise reasoning as an explanation containing assertions about causes and consequences. To evaluate reasoning we identify reasoning violations, i.e., claims that do not follow from the explanation's input. We propose a taxonomy of violations (\cref{fig:arch}) and a way to detect them automatically. Our taxonomy splits unfaithful reasoning (\cref{fig:arch}, bottom) into five violation types (\cref{tab:severity}):
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{V1 Fabricated:} a fact or entity without evidence.
  \item \textbf{V2 Contradicted:} the opposite of a fact.
  \item \textbf{V3 Over-generalised:} the correct category without specific details.
  \item \textbf{V4 Under-specified:} an assertion that should be made is omitted.
  \item \textbf{V5 Incoherent:} two statements contradict each other.
\end{itemize}

A fabricated entity is high severity as it may prompt the deployer to consider unrelated traffic. Contradicting ``normal'' behaviour may hide attacks; inventing ``malicious'' behaviour may cause disruption. Hence, V1, V2 and V5 are high severity. Missing details or assertions (V3, V4) are low severity in isolation, medium severity if both types are present. Discrete violations are more actionable for deployers than opaque scores.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Severity mapping of the violation taxonomy.}
\label{tab:severity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
Violation & Meaning & Severity \\
\midrule
V1 & Fabricated       & High \\
V2 & Contradicted     & High \\
V3 & Over-generalised & Low \\
V4 & Under-specified  & Low \\
V3 $\wedge$ V4 & Both omission modes & Medium \\
V5 & Incoherent       & High \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Architectural overview}
\rg{} is deterministic and auditable by design. The system consists of three loosely coupled components: a formal layer producing a formal bound; an AI layer using the bound to create a prompt and obtain natural language from an LLM; and a verification layer validating the explanation and producing a list of violations (\cref{fig:arch}). The formal and verification layers implement our taxonomy (\cref{fig:arch}, top), while the AI layer is a swappable interface to a range of LLMs.

\subsection{Formal bound}
We generate explanations of IEC-104 protocol frames. The formal layer consumes raw frames and outputs a formal bound, a JSON object (\cref{lst:bound}) containing metadata (event id, timestamp), observable features (protocol, direction, endpoints, ASDU fields), allowed/required/forbidden claim sets, and \texttt{machine\_constraints} flags. Constraints are Booleans asserting facts an LLM is unlikely to know. If no automaton is found, \rg{} assumes no constraints (all false) and fails closed, labelling artifacts proxy. Deployers should not show proxy explanations or interpret them as approval.

\lstinputlisting[caption={Canonical formal bound (proxy schema v2.1); the \texttt{allowed}/\texttt{required}/\texttt{forbidden} claim lists are abbreviated.}, label={lst:bound}, float=t]{bound_listing.txt}

\subsection{Violation detection}
\rg{} extracts claims from explanations, compares them to the formal bound, and classifies violations. Claim extraction uses spaCy for POS-tagging and named-entity recognition (NER). We add a custom \texttt{EntityRuler} matching ICS-domain patterns to improve performance on the limited ICS vocabulary: the ruler deterministically recognises closed categories before delegating open categories (e.g.\ numbers) to spaCy's statistical model. Bound comparison marks each claim \textsc{supported}, \textsc{unsupported}, or \textsc{contradicted} based on whether it appears in the formal bound. \rg{} checks directions and endpoints against the bound, compares \texttt{machine\_constraints} to claim entities, and verifies protocol numbers against the hard-coded constants 104 and 2404. Violation classification uses two complementary methods: a lexicon flags some violation types conservatively (always on), while syntactic analysis using spaCy dependency parsing flags others. Both ignore negated or meta-referential mentions (e.g.\ no alert is raised). A substring-based fallback provides the same checks when spaCy is unavailable. These techniques are simple but deterministic, as the same explanation and bound always produce the same output. This supports auditing and reproducibility, sacrificing recall on unseen claims.

% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\noindent\textbf{Dataset and proxy labels.}
We use the \texttt{eon-iec} subset of the publicly available \emph{ICS Dataset for Smart Grid Anomaly Detection}~\cite{MatousekRysavyGrofcik2022SmartGrid} from Brno University of Technology: 20,000 atomic IEC-104 rows, losslessly transformed into bounds. Each row carries a proxy label with one of four event types---normal polling, function-code violation, network scan, and topology change---observed in this sample; the fifth type (replay attack) is left to the Modbus dataset~\cite{RysavyMatousek2021Modbus}.

\noindent\textbf{Models and parameters.}
We evaluate three open-source models, \texttt{llama3.1:8b}, \texttt{mistral:7b}, and \texttt{phi3:mini}, run locally through Ollama with default (4-bit CPU) quantization, as well as two proprietary APIs: \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} and \texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite}. To isolate differences between models, all calls fix sampling at temperature $0.1$ and top-$p$ $0.9$.

\noindent\textbf{Prompts and sampling.}
All models receive the same verbose system prompt, which (i) restricts them to what the bounds support; (ii) prohibits inventing attack types or speculating on attacker identity, intent, impact, causation, mitigation, etc.; and (iii) encourages them to say when they are not sure. This design avoids prompt-engineering confounds and makes reported violations a lower bound on each model's violations. We stratify the dataset by proxy label and sample 25 prompts per event type. We exclude hosts since they are obfuscated as IDs. In total, each model receives 100 prompts.

\noindent\textbf{Metrics and targets.}
For each class (including ``no violation''), we report precision, recall, and F1, as well as aggregated macro-F1 and Cohen's $\kappa$. We also report accuracy. We pre-register targets of $\kappa \ge 0.70$ (substantial agreement) and macro-F1 $\ge 0.80$ to establish \rg's reliability before relying on it for analyses. Where informative, we also perform chi-square tests of independence to determine whether two categorical variables are associated, for example, violation category, collapsed into fewer buckets, with model or event type.

\noindent\textbf{Ground truth.}
We collect labels on a sample of 200 (bound, explanation) pairs. To avoid confirmation bias, the annotator does not see \rg's violation labels. Our goal was a random sample of violations across categories; however, given the small sample size and interest in rare categories such as hallucinated types, we oversample violations and underrepresent clean samples.

In a pilot annotation study, a single annotator labeled all samples in succession. This marathon annotation study was very noisy, with $\kappa \approx 0$ against \rg's labels, likely due to annotator fatigue. We discard those labels and restart with breaks every 25 samples. For a second-rater check, we sent a separate 50 samples to a domain expert at Brno University of Technology.

\noindent\textbf{Suite overview.}
In total, we evaluate 1,000 responses. Half are synthetic, hand-constructed responses used only for regression-testing \rg{} itself; these responses intentionally exhibit all four violations (V1--V4) alongside clean responses. The remaining responses come from our IEC dataset: 300 responses from the three local models and 200 responses from the two cloud-based models. We use the regression-test results to verify that \rg{} works as expected, but do not count them toward model-level conclusions.

% ============================================================
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

We now discuss the results of this study. First, we analyse the distribution of violations in the LM's responses. Then, we analyse the results from \rg{}, the event-based hallucination detector, comparing them against a smaller set of human-annotated data. We perform chi-square tests of independence to understand the relationship between event types and violation types. Finally, we discuss the latency of our approach.

\subsection{Distribution of violation types}
\Cref{tab:permodel} shows the distribution of violation types for each of the tested language models. In general, we observe an order-of-magnitude difference between models with the most (\texttt{llama3.1:8b} which had 63\%) and least (\texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite} which had 0\%) clean (NONE) responses. In general, larger local models performed better, but alignment and decoding strategies had a big impact on the cloud-based LMs. Some qualitative differences between models include \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} violating constraints in the form of high-severity hallucination (V1) the most, \texttt{gemini-2.5-flash-lite} never hallucinating but always under-specifying (V4), and \texttt{phi3:mini} hallucinating and underspecifying often and producing incoherent responses frequently. Overall, given sufficiently stringent prompts, LMs are more likely to hallucinate facts (V1) rather than reversing them (V2), and to misspecify by omission (V4) rather than overgeneralisation (V3).

